    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mitanni was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> great power in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with a Hurrian population and kings bearing Indo-Aryan names.</li><li>Famed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warfare, it rivalled and later allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sealing ties through royal marriages and letters.</li><li>A murdered king and contested succession let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> carve it up; Assyria annexed the remnant by c. 1250 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Mitanni treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> calls on the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varuna-river">Varuna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashvins">Nasatyas</a>, some of the earliest evidence for Indo-Aryan languages anywhere.</p></aside>
    </section>

